# Packaging satellite for Debian and Ubuntu

Written for a maintainer who has not done this before. Nothing here changes how
satellite builds; the packaging drives the ordinary top-level Makefile.


## Build it

Install the build dependencies once:

    sudo apt build-dep .

(or, if that fails because the source package is not registered yet:
`sudo apt install debhelper g++ libgtk-4-dev libvte-2.91-gtk4-dev pkg-config`)

Then, from the top of the source tree — the directory that contains `Makefile`
and `debian/`, not from inside `debian/`:

    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

  * `-b` means binary only: build the `.deb` files and skip the source package.
    This is what you want while iterating, and the only mode that needs no
    upstream tarball (see "Source format" below).
  * `-us -uc` mean do not sign the source and do not sign the changes file. You
    only sign when uploading to an archive.

The results land in the *parent* directory, which surprises everyone once:

    ../satellite_0.1.0-1_amd64.deb
    ../satellite-term_0.1.0-1_amd64.deb
    ../satellite-dbgsym_0.1.0-1_amd64.ddeb
    ../satellite-term-dbgsym_0.1.0-1_amd64.ddeb
    ../satellite_0.1.0-1_amd64.changes

The two `.ddeb` files are the debug symbols, split out automatically because
`debian/rules` builds with `-g`. They are optional to install and useful in a
backtrace.


## Look at it before installing it

    dpkg -c ../satellite_0.1.0-1_amd64.deb    # every file, with its mode
    dpkg -I ../satellite_0.1.0-1_amd64.deb    # the control data: Depends etc.

The single most important check in this whole package is that the interpreter
did not acquire a GUI dependency:

    dpkg -I ../satellite_0.1.0-1_amd64.deb | grep Depends

That line must name only `libc6`, `libgcc-s1` and `libstdc++6`. If `libgtk-4-1`
or `libvte-2.91-gtk4-0` ever appears there, the binary split described in
DESIGN.md section 9 has been undone somewhere in the Makefile and the headless
install has silently regressed.

If you have `lintian`, run it — it is the policy checker, and its complaints
are usually right:

    lintian ../satellite_0.1.0-1_amd64.changes


## Install and remove

    sudo apt install ../satellite_0.1.0-1_amd64.deb
    sudo apt install ../satellite-term_0.1.0-1_amd64.deb   # only for the GUI

Use `apt install ./path.deb` rather than `dpkg -i`, because apt will pull the
dependencies and `dpkg` will merely complain that they are missing.

    sudo apt remove satellite-term satellite

After installing, the acceptance test for the whole install design is that this
works with a clean environment and no variables set:

    env -u SATELLITE_PATH \
        satl --run /usr/share/satellite/examples/hello_world.satl


## prefix and DESTDIR, which are not the same thing

These two are the only part of packaging worth understanding properly, and the
difference is what makes a package relocatable-by-construction rather than by
luck.

**`prefix` is where the software will live when it is installed.** It is a
promise baked into the build. The package sets `prefix=/usr`, and that value
reaches the compiler: the third and last entry in the library search order is
compiled in as `-DSATELLITE_LIB_DIR`, so with `prefix=/usr` the interpreter
carries the string `/usr/share/satellite/lib` inside it. Build with the default
`prefix=/usr/local` and install to `/usr` anyway, and you get a binary that
looks in a directory the package never created. That is why `debian/rules`
passes `prefix=/usr` to the *build* step and not only to the install step.

**`DESTDIR` is a staging directory, and it is a lie the build is told exactly
once.** `make install DESTDIR=/some/scratch` writes to
`/some/scratch/usr/bin/satl` instead of `/usr/bin/satl`, so files can
be collected as an ordinary user without touching the real filesystem.
`DESTDIR` is prepended when the files are *written* and appears nowhere in
what is written: not in a compiled-in path, not in a script, not in the file
names recorded in the `.deb`. `dpkg-deb` then packs the staging tree, and the
paths in the archive are `usr/bin/satl` again.

So: `prefix` is baked in, `DESTDIR` never is. That distinction is why one build
of satellite can be packed into a `.deb`, a tarball and an `apt` archive
without being rebuilt, and why `debian/rules` may pass `DESTDIR` freely but
must pass `prefix` carefully.

Here `DESTDIR` is `debian/tmp`. Both packages are installed into that one tree
and then split by `debian/satellite.install` and
`debian/satellite-term.install`, which list the paths each package claims.
Anything landing in `debian/tmp` that neither file claims makes the build fail
— `dh_missing` is strict by default in debhelper compat 13 — and that is
deliberate: a new installed file must be assigned to a package on purpose,
rather than forgotten into oblivion.


## Source format

`debian/source/format` says `3.0 (quilt)`, for two reasons.

The deciding one is arithmetic: the version is `0.1.0-1`, and the `-1` is a
Debian revision, meaning "the first packaging of upstream 0.1.0". A `3.0
(native)` package is one where upstream and packaging are the same release with
no separate revision, and its version may not contain a hyphen at all; with
`3.0 (native)` the version would have to be plain `0.1.0`. The requested
version therefore selects quilt.

The better reason is that it keeps the two version streams apart. A packaging
fix — a corrected dependency, a changed install path — becomes `0.1.0-2` and
does not pretend the language changed. That separation is worth having from the
first upload rather than retrofitted at the first bug.

The cost is that a full source build wants an upstream tarball beside the tree:

    cd ..
    make -C satellite clean      # do not tar .o files and stale binaries
    tar --exclude=debian -czf satellite_0.1.0.orig.tar.gz satellite
    cd satellite
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc        # no -b: builds source and binary

Binary-only builds (`-b`, above) never look for that tarball, so day-to-day
work needs none of this.


## What each file in debian/ does

    control              package metadata: names, dependencies, descriptions.
                         One "Source:" stanza plus one "Package:" stanza per
                         binary package produced.
    rules                an executable makefile. `dh $@` runs the standard
                         sequence; the override_* targets replace single steps.
    changelog            the version number lives here and nowhere else. Its
                         topmost entry is what the build names the .deb after.
    copyright            machine-readable licensing, a paragraph per file set.
                         Its licence text is a transcription of the top-level
                         LICENSE, which is the same text `make install` puts at
                         share/doc/satellite/copyright; the two say the same
                         thing and have to keep saying it.
    satellite.install    which staged paths go into the satellite package.
    satellite-term.install    the same for satellite-term.
    satellite.dirs       directories to create that contain no files.
                         /usr/share/satellite/lib is empty today: it is the
                         compiled-in SATELLITE_LIB_DIR, and it has to exist so
                         that dropping a .satl file into it is all a user has
                         to do. An .install file cannot carry an empty
                         directory, which is why this file exists at all.
    source/format        3.0 (quilt), discussed above.


## Changing the version

Do not edit the version anywhere but `debian/changelog`, and prefer the tool:

    dch -v 0.1.1-1 "what changed"
    dch -r                            # finalise the entry for release

`dch` fills in the date and the trailer in the exact format the parser wants; a
hand-edited trailer with a weekday that does not match its date is a build
failure with an unhelpful message.


## What debian/rules requires of the top-level Makefile

The packaging drives the upstream Makefile rather than reimplementing it, so it
depends on that Makefile keeping to the agreed install contract. If a build
fails with `Cannot find (any matches for) "usr/share/..."`, one of these is not
being honoured:

  * `make install prefix=/usr DESTDIR=<dir>` exists and stages the whole tree.
  * `prefix` reaches the compiler as `-DSATELLITE_LIB_DIR`, so the value has to
    be given to the build as well as to the install. `debian/rules` passes it
    to both.
  * The staged tree is exactly the ten paths listed in `satellite.install`,
    `satellite-term.install` and `satellite.dirs`. Anything else staged fails
    the build under `dh_missing`, and anything listed but not staged fails
    under `dh_install`; either failure is the packaging noticing that the contract
    moved, and the fix is to update the `.install` file rather than to silence
    the check.
  * The man pages, the `.desktop` file, the icon and the bash completion have
    sources in the tree for `make install` to install. They are upstream files,
    not packaging files, because a `make install` into `/usr/local` on a machine
    that has never heard of dpkg should produce a working man page too.
  * `CXXFLAGS` may be replaced whole. `debian/rules` restates the flags the
    build genuinely requires — `-std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra` — and appends
    `dpkg-buildflags`', which is only safe because `-DSATELLITE_LIB_DIR` is a
    literal on the `system.o` recipe line and not part of any variable. Move it
    into `CXXFLAGS` upstream and the define vanishes on the next package build.
  * Both link rules read `$(LDFLAGS)` and the Makefile assigns it nothing, so
    dpkg's link-time hardening reaches the link through the environment
    `buildflags.mk` exports. A link rule that stops mentioning `$(LDFLAGS)`
    silently drops relro and bindnow.
  * Changing `prefix` recompiles `system.o`. The Makefile records the value in
    `.libdir-stamp` and depends on it, so `dh_auto_build` retargeting a tree
    somebody already built by hand still produces a binary that names
    `/usr/share/satellite/lib`.

`satellite.install` matches the man pages with `satl.1*` rather than
`satl.1.gz`, so it does not care whether upstream compresses them or
leaves that to `dh_compress`. Both end up gzipped in the `.deb`.


## Known rough edges

  * **The test suite is slow.** `make test` builds eleven test binaries, each
    recompiling the whole runtime and compiler, and one of them under
    ThreadSanitizer. Nothing needs a display. `DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b` skips it while iterating on packaging.

    On an architecture with no ThreadSanitizer — anything that is not amd64,
    arm64, mips64el, ppc64el, riscv64 or s390x — `debian/rules` passes upstream
    `TSAN=0`, which drops `library_test_tsan` and runs the other ten. Without
    it `-fsanitize=thread` would fail to compile and take the whole build with
    it, on architectures the package promises to build on by declaring
    `Architecture: any`.

  * **example/ is a working directory.** It contains built comparison drivers,
    and `example/website/gtk_window` is a stale binary no clean target removes.
    `debian/rules` deletes them from the staging tree after install, because an
    architecture-dependent file under `/usr/share` is a policy violation. The
    deletions are `rm -f`, so they cost nothing once upstream's install rule
    filters the directory itself.
